 "description": "<p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6000003814697px;\"><strong>Greenland GPS Network (GNET)</strong></p><p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6000003814697px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 1.125em; line-height: 1.2em;\">56 GPS stations are located along the Greenland ice sheet. Together the stations constitute the Greenland GPS Network (GNET) and are operated by DTU Space.</span></p><p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6000003814697px;\">The stations measure elevation changes in Greenland&#39;s crust that can be seen as an expression of the ice sheet&#39;s mass becomes larger or smaller.</p><p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6000003814697px;\">The measurements from GNET stations makes it possible to follow the variations in the earth&#39;s crust over a period of several years and thus it is possible to determine whether there is a net ice cap loss.</p>",
